## Scope

Sorokeep signs webhook payloads with HMAC-SHA256 in an X-Sorokeep-Signature header. Verification uses Sorokeep''s own exported verifyWebhookSignature — reimplementing HMAC here is explicitly forbidden and is the mistake the TypeScript backend decision exists to prevent. Note that this is blocked until E07-01 resolves the missing export.

## Files

- `lumens-vault/backend/src/adapters/sorokeep-webhook/verify.ts (new)`
- `lumens-vault/backend/test/adapters/sorokeep-webhook/verify.test.ts (new)`

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] verifyWebhookSignature is imported from sorokeep; no HMAC implementation exists in this repository
- [ ] Verification runs against the raw request body bytes, not a re-serialised object — JSON round-tripping changes bytes and breaks the signature, and this is the most common way such checks silently fail open
- [ ] A valid signature passes; a tampered body, a wrong secret, a missing header and a malformed header value all fail
- [ ] The secret comes from configuration and never appears in logs or error messages
- [ ] A test proves a re-serialised body fails, documenting why raw bytes are required

## Non-goals

The endpoint is E10-09.

